The following files under `fixtures/` are NOT snapshots. They are generated by
this repository and are signed with throwaway keys whose seeds are published in
the generators that produce them (`tools/make-throwaway-fixtures.mjs`,
`tools/make-acta-fixtures.mjs`):

  fixtures/verification-state/synthetic/
  fixtures/verification-state/mappings/v0.3.0-2026-05-30.json
  fixtures/acta/synthetic/
  fixtures/acta/keys/
  fixtures/keys/
  fixtures/x402-pr-2853-compliance-fields/numbers/
    Generated by `tools/compliance-fields-recompute.ts --write-numbers`, unsigned
    and keyless, and so an exception to the sentence above about throwaway keys.

Those keys are throwaways by design. Anyone can re-derive them from the
published seeds, which is why they prove nothing about anyone and must never be
treated as identity material.

Note also that `fixtures/verification-state/spec-examples/jwks-fixture-detached.json`
is an upstream snapshot that contains BOTH halves of an Ed25519 fixture keypair.
Its publisher committed the private half deliberately, so that the detached
sample fixture can be regenerated byte-for-byte by any implementer; the file
states this in its own comment. It is fixture material, not production key
material, and no production private key appears anywhere in this repository.

Both files carrying private key bytes — `fixtures/keys/test-throwaway-ed25519.seed.txt`
and `fixtures/verification-state/spec-examples/jwks-fixture-detached.json` — were
reviewed on 2026-07-29 and retained deliberately: the first is throwaway by
construction, its seeds decoding to their own labels, and the second is upstream's
deliberate fixture-reproducibility choice whose removal would break the byte-pinning
that makes the snapshot meaningful.

